<div class="article-body">
<h2>Program Overview</h2>
<p>This 4-week program adds 5 seconds per set each week to your baseline hold. A 15 to 20 second baseline reaches roughly 30 seconds by test day. Repeat weeks 3 and 4 until you reach 30 if you start lower. You train 3 days per week with 3 sets per session.</p>
<p>Weekly hang time stays under 5 minutes for a baseline up to 20 seconds. The program suits anyone who can hold the bar for about 5 seconds. No previous training experience is needed. The doses are common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Detail</th><th>Specification</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Goal</strong></td><td>Build toward a 30-second dead hang</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Duration</strong></td><td>4 weeks</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Frequency</strong></td><td>3 sessions per week</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Sets per Session</strong></td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Rest Between Sets</strong></td><td>90 seconds</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Grip</strong></td><td>Double overhand, shoulder width (neutral grip once in week 3)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2>Before You Start</h2>
<h3>Equipment Needed</h3>
<p>A pull-up bar that holds your full body weight is the only requirement. Doorframe bars, wall-mounted bars or gym pull-up stations all work. The how to dead hang guide has setup details.</p>
<p>Optional additions include chalk if sweat ends your hang. A timer app on your phone or the timer and log on this site both work. Step down rather than drop; a mat helps.</p>
<h3>Baseline Test</h3>
<p>Grip the bar with a double overhand grip at shoulder width. Hang with straight arms and start your timer. Hang until a second or two before your grip gives out. Then step down under control. Record that number in seconds. Your baseline hold is about 80% of that number. It is the hold you can repeat for three clean sets and it is your Week 1 target.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Score under 5 seconds?</strong> Start with the regression exercises in the <a href="/beginner-deadhang/">beginner dead hang guide</a> for 1-2 weeks before beginning this program.</p></div>
<h2>Week 1: Foundation</h2>
<p>Week 1 establishes your baseline volume and locks in proper dead hang form. Every set uses your baseline hold. That is about 80% of your test-day max, so all three sets stay clean. Do not push beyond that number this week.</p>
<h3>Session Structure</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Set 1:</strong> Hang for your baseline hold</li>
<li><strong>Rest:</strong> 90 seconds</li>
<li><strong>Set 2:</strong> Hang for your baseline hold</li>
<li><strong>Rest:</strong> 90 seconds</li>
<li><strong>Set 3:</strong> Hang for your baseline hold</li>
</ul>
<h3>Form Checklist</h3>
<p>Grip the bar with thumbs wrapped around it. Keep arms fully extended. Pack your shoulders down slightly away from your ears. Breathe steadily through the nose. Look straight ahead or slightly down.</p>
<p>Record all three set times and how the last set felt.</p>
<h2>Week 2: Building Endurance</h2>
<p>Add 5 seconds to each set compared to your Week 1 times. A 15-second baseline means a 20-second target per set in Week 2. Rest stays at 90 seconds between sets.</p>
<h3>Key Focus</h3>
<p>Grip fatigue hits harder this week. Your forearms may burn earlier in each set. Keep breathing steady and maintain shoulder position. Do not let your shoulders shrug up toward your ears.</p>
<p>The third set becomes genuinely difficult. Falling 2 to 3 seconds short of target is acceptable. Record your actual times and aim for full target times by your next session.</p>
<h2>Week 3: Pushing Through</h2>
<p>Add another 5 seconds per set on top of Week 2. Your target sets now run 10 seconds longer than your original baseline. Rest stays at 90 seconds.</p>
<h3>Introduce Grip Variety</h3>
<p>On one session this week swap your overhand grip for a neutral grip (palms facing each other) if your bar allows it. Neutral grip gives your fingers and forearms a slightly different stimulus. Return to overhand for the other two sessions.</p>
<p>Mental resistance appears this week. Your mind may signal fatigue before your grip actually gives out. Practice breathing through the discomfort. Focus on one breath at a time instead of watching the clock.</p>
<h2>Week 4: Testing</h2>
<p>The final week builds toward a max test on the last session.</p>
<h3>Sessions 1 and 2</h3>
<p>Perform your normal 3 sets at Week 3 target times with 90 seconds rest between sets. Keep form strict. These sessions maintain volume without draining you before the final test.</p>
<h3>Session 3: Max Test Day</h3>
<ol>
<li>Warm up with a short mobility routine and 2 easy hangs of 10 to 15 seconds each</li>
<li>Rest 2-3 minutes</li>
<li>Grip the bar and hang for your best controlled hold. Step down a second or two before your grip gives out.</li>
<li>Record your result</li>
</ol>
<p>Target: 30 seconds or longer. Test day shows where you are now. It is not a fixed outcome. Compare it with your baseline from Day 1.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Hit 30 seconds?</strong> The <a href="/8-week-program/">8-week plan framework</a> suits a comfortable 20 to 30 second hold. Fell short? Repeat weeks 3 and 4 of this program until you reach 30 seconds.</p></div>
<h2>Daily Schedule</h2>
<p>Follow this Monday/Wednesday/Friday schedule. Shift the days if needed but always leave one rest day between sessions.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Week</th><th>Monday</th><th>Wednesday</th><th>Friday</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Week 1</strong></td><td>3 &times; baseline, 90s rest</td><td>3 &times; baseline, 90s rest</td><td>3 &times; baseline, 90s rest</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Week 2</strong></td><td>3 &times; (baseline + 5s), 90s rest</td><td>3 &times; (baseline + 5s), 90s rest</td><td>3 &times; (baseline + 5s), 90s rest</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Week 3</strong></td><td>3 &times; (baseline + 10s), 90s rest</td><td>3 &times; (baseline + 10s), neutral grip, 90s rest</td><td>3 &times; (baseline + 10s), 90s rest</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Week 4</strong></td><td>3 &times; Week 3 target, 90s rest</td><td>3 &times; Week 3 target, 90s rest</td><td>2 warm-up hangs + 1 test hold</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Sample Schedule for a 10-Second Baseline</h3>
<p>A 10-second baseline reaches 20 seconds per set by week 3. Repeat weeks 3 and 4 to keep building toward 30.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Week</th><th>Target Per Set</th><th>Total Hang Time Per Session</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Week 1</strong></td><td>10 seconds</td><td>30 seconds</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Week 2</strong></td><td>15 seconds</td><td>45 seconds</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Week 3</strong></td><td>20 seconds</td><td>60 seconds</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Week 4</strong></td><td>20 seconds (test day: best controlled hold)</td><td>60 seconds + test hold</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Prefer to log your own numbers as you go instead of a fixed table? Use the <a href="/training-log/">timer and training log</a> to enter your own hold and rest settings and track each session.</p>
<h2>What Comes Next</h2>
<p>This site treats 30 seconds as the point where the beginner plan ends and the intermediate plan starts. Two paths open from here.</p>
<h3>Path 1: 8-Week Plan Framework</h3>
<p>The 8-week plan framework suits a comfortable 20 to 30 second hold. It uses two four-week blocks. Review points compare like-for-like sessions and change one variable at a time.</p>
<h3>Path 2: Maintain and Integrate</h3>
<p>Keep your 30-second hold with 2 sessions per week at 3 sets each. Short hangs also fit alongside climbing or general gym training. The <a href="/intermediate-deadhang/">intermediate dead hang guide</a> covers the next level when you are ready to progress further.</p>
